---
import Base           from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Nav            from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer         from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import SectionEyebrow from '../../components/SectionEyebrow.astro';
import '../../styles/global.css';

// Installation documentation for Knot: one page, every OS, in the style
// of a distro's install guide. Requirements, download, verify, install,
// first run, update, uninstall. Deep-linkable per-OS sections.
//
// Editorial rules that apply here:
//  - The Ubuntu App-Center limitation, the not-notarized Gatekeeper step
//    and the Windows status are stated up front. Someone who hits one of
//    them with no warning writes an email; someone who was told about it
//    reads one more line and carries on.
//  - Commands are copy-ready: one command per block, no prose inside.
//  - This page carries the DEPTH so the product page's download section
//    can stay lean (de-bloat campaign, 2026-07-28).
//
// Open questions for the owner. These live HERE, in the frontmatter, and
// nowhere in the markup below: this block is compiled away, while an HTML
// comment in the template is emitted verbatim into the built page (there is
// no compressor integration in astro.config.mjs). Nothing bracketed may sit
// in rendered copy either; a reader is not the audience for a note to self.
//  - CONFIRM: the Ubuntu release in which App Center stopped installing
//    local .deb files, and whether this was found by handing someone the .deb.
//  - CONFIRM: the auto-commit debounce, in seconds, as shipped.
//  - ANSWERED from the source, 2026-09-18: auto-merge DOES clear a diverged,
//    conflict-free push. knot-core/src/repo.rs auto_merge_incoming() returns
//    Merged with a deterministic commit (sorted parents, fixed identity,
//    max timestamp); it is driven from knot-daemon auto_commit.rs:295 and the
//    1 Hz sweep in reconcile.rs:58. Only Conflict, ProtectedBranchHeld and
//    Deferred stay parked. Section 05 updated.
//  - CONFIRM: the macOS and Ubuntu versions this page was last walked
//    through, and the date, if a "last checked" footer line is wanted back.
//  - CONFIRM: the Tor-vs-LAN sync time, with the build it was measured on,
//    before any number goes into section 05.
//  - OWNER DECISION: first person "I" proposed for the other-distros line
//    ("tell me which one"). Not shipped without a yes.

const DEB_FILE  = 'knot-vcs_0.1.0+20260826.2319.3fff7c87a434_amd64.deb';
const DEB_SHA   = '7e64c9aebca4b57a9e4e9ec0bff2e517ace921681b7da1779aa7c03d6223f938';
const DMG_FILE  = 'Knot-0.1.0+20260826.2319.3fff7c87a-macOS.dmg';
const DMG_SHA   = '8eb0f6d2d57340e379c878b63077a1a4cb37963e9d7d44764e6a7c1deab9aa84';
const REL_BASE  = 'https://github.com/pointbreaklab-hub/knot/releases/latest/download';
---

<Base
  title="Install Knot · macOS, Linux, Windows"
  description="Installing Knot on macOS 13 or newer and on Debian/Ubuntu with glibc 2.31+: download, checksum, install, start the daemon, first synced save. No Windows build yet."
>
  <Nav />

  <main class="min-h-screen pt-24 pb-24 px-6">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto relative">

      <div aria-hidden="true" class="absolute top-0 left-1/2 -translate-x-1/2 w-[60%] h-64 bg-accent/[0.06] blur-3xl pointer-events-none"></div>

      <SectionEyebrow num="00" label="DOCUMENTATION · INSTALL" align="center" />

      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-black text-primary text-center mb-4 tracking-tight">
        Installing Knot
      </h1>
      <p class="text-secondary text-center max-w-xl mx-auto mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Download to first synced save, per operating system: macOS and Debian/Ubuntu
        only. If you are on Windows, section 03 is the whole answer and it is short.
      </p>
      <p class="text-hint text-center text-xs font-mono mb-14">
        <a href="#macos"   class="text-accent hover:underline">macOS</a> ·
        <a href="#linux"   class="text-accent hover:underline">Linux</a> ·
        <a href="#windows" class="text-accent hover:underline">Windows</a> ·
        <a href="#verify"  class="text-accent hover:underline">Verify a download</a> ·
        <a href="#first-save" class="text-accent hover:underline">First save</a>
      </p>

      <!-- ── 01 macOS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section id="macos" class="relative mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <SectionEyebrow num="01" label="MACOS" align="left" />
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-primary mb-4">macOS</h2>

        <div class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed space-y-4">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface border border-border font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">
            <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">Requires</span><span class="text-primary/80">macOS 13 (Ventura) or newer</span></div>
            <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">CPU</span><span class="text-primary/80">Intel natively · Apple Silicon via Rosetta 2</span></div>
            <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">Ships with</span><span class="text-primary/80">daemon, desktop app, CLI, Tor; self-contained, nothing to install first</span></div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">1. Download</h3>
            <p>Get the disk image from the <a href={`${REL_BASE}/Knot-macos.dmg`} class="text-accent hover:underline">latest release</a> (also on the <a href="/knot/#download" class="text-accent hover:underline">download page</a>). Checking the checksum first is optional: <a href="#verify" class="text-accent hover:underline">steps below</a>.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">2. Install</h3>
            <p>Open the .dmg, read the bundled <span class="text-primary/80 font-mono">INSTALL.txt</span>, and drag <span class="text-primary/80">Knot</span> into <span class="text-primary/80">Applications</span>.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">3. First open: the one-time Gatekeeper step</h3>
            <p class="mb-2">Knot is signed but not yet Apple-notarized, so macOS blocks the very first open. The xattr command strips the quarantine flag before you ever double-click, whereas the System Settings route only offers you Open Anyway once macOS has already refused to open Knot and you have gone looking for the reason. Clear it once, any of these ways:</p>
            <p class="mb-1 text-xs font-mono text-hint uppercase tracking-widest">Most reliable, any macOS version</p>
            <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface border border-border rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed text-xs mb-2"><code>xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine "/Applications/Knot.app"</code></pre>
            <ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1 text-secondary/90">
              <li>Or: open Knot once (it gets blocked), then <span class="text-primary/80">System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → "Open Anyway"</span>.</li>
              <li>Older macOS (pre-Sequoia): right-click Knot → Open → Open.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">4. First run</h3>
            <p>Open Knot. The three-step welcome shows your new device identity (no account, no password: an Ed25519 key generated on your machine) and ends on <span class="text-primary/80">Protect a folder</span>. Pick any folder; every save in it becomes a real git commit. The daemon starts automatically with the app and keeps running in the background.</p>
          </div>

          <details class="group p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface border border-border">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center gap-3 text-sm font-bold text-primary">
              <span class="text-accent font-mono transition-transform group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
              Updating · uninstalling · where things live
            </summary>
            <div class="text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed space-y-2 mt-3">
              <p><span class="text-primary/80 font-bold">Update:</span> download the new .dmg and drag Knot to Applications again, replacing the old copy. Your projects, identity, and pairings are untouched: they live outside the app bundle.</p>
              <p><span class="text-primary/80 font-bold">Uninstall:</span> quit Knot, delete <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">/Applications/Knot.app</span>. Your repositories stay where they are. They are plain folders with real git history, readable by any git tool, with or without Knot.</p>
              <p><span class="text-primary/80 font-bold">Data locations:</span> identity and settings under <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">~/Library/Application Support/knot/</span>; your code stays exactly where your folders are.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 02 Linux ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section id="linux" class="relative mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <SectionEyebrow num="02" label="LINUX" align="left" />
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-primary mb-4">Linux (Debian / Ubuntu)</h2>

        <div class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed space-y-4">
          <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface border border-border font-mono text-xs space-y-1.5">
            <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">Requires</span><span class="text-primary/80">Debian/Ubuntu on x86_64, glibc 2.31+ (Ubuntu 20.04+, Debian 11+)</span></div>
            <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">Depends</span><span class="text-primary/80">git (installed automatically by apt if missing)</span></div>
            <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-28 shrink-0">Ships with</span><span class="text-primary/80">daemon, desktop app, CLI, Tor, on-device AI runtime; self-contained</span></div>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">1. Download</h3>
            <p>Get the package from the <a href={`${REL_BASE}/knot-vcs-amd64.deb`} class="text-accent hover:underline">latest release</a>, then optionally <a href="#verify" class="text-accent hover:underline">verify the checksum</a>.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">2. Install (one command)</h3>
            <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface border border-border rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed text-xs mb-2"><code>sudo apt install ./knot-vcs-amd64.deb</code></pre>
            <p class="text-xs text-secondary/90 leading-relaxed">The leading <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">./</span> tells apt this is a local file; apt resolves the git dependency for you. <span class="text-primary/80">Double-clicking the .deb will not install it.</span> Current Ubuntu ships no GUI that installs local .deb files: App&nbsp;Center is snap-only by design, and this affects every vendor's .deb, not just Knot.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">3. Start the daemon</h3>
            <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface border border-border rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed text-xs mb-2"><code>systemctl --user daemon-reload
systemctl --user enable --now knot-vcs</code></pre>
            <p class="text-xs text-secondary/90">The package registers the service for every user; this turns it on for yours, now and on every login.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h3 class="text-primary font-bold mb-1.5">4. First run</h3>
            <p class="mb-2">Launch <span class="text-primary/80">Knot</span> from your app launcher (or run <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">knot-app</span>). The same three-step welcome as macOS. Or, from a terminal in any folder:</p>
            <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface border border-border rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed text-xs"><code>knot solo</code></pre>
            <p class="text-xs text-secondary/90 mt-2">turns on watch + auto-commit + sync for that folder.</p>
          </div>

          <details class="group p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface border border-border">
            <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none flex items-center gap-3 text-sm font-bold text-primary">
              <span class="text-accent font-mono transition-transform group-open:rotate-45">+</span>
              Updating · uninstalling · health check · other distros
            </summary>
            <div class="text-xs text-secondary leading-relaxed space-y-2 mt-3">
              <p><span class="text-primary/80 font-bold">Update:</span> download the newer .deb and run <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">sudo apt install ./…</span> again. apt upgrades in place; your daemon, projects, and pairings carry over.</p>
              <p><span class="text-primary/80 font-bold">Uninstall:</span> <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">sudo apt remove knot-vcs</span>. The package owns nothing inside your project folders; each one is still a git repository afterwards, readable with plain git.</p>
              <p><span class="text-primary/80 font-bold">Health check:</span> <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">knot doctor</span>.</p>
              <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface border border-border rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed text-xs"><code>  ✓ daemon reachable
  ⚠ Tor onion not published yet (still bootstrapping, or started without --tor)
  ○ no LAN detected (Tor-only)</code></pre>
              <p>Each line has its own condition, so a healthy run prints the first and neither of the others. The Tor warning is normal in the first minutes after a cold start.</p>
              <p><span class="text-primary/80 font-bold">Other distros (Fedora, Arch, …):</span> not packaged yet. The .deb is the only Linux artifact today; a store-grade snap/flatpak is under consideration. If you need Knot on another distro now, <a href="mailto:mail.roshankumargupta@gmail.com?subject=Knot%20on%20another%20distro" class="text-accent hover:underline">say which one</a>. Real demand moves it up the list.</p>
            </div>
          </details>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 03 Windows ───────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section id="windows" class="relative mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <SectionEyebrow num="03" label="WINDOWS" align="left" />
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-primary mb-4">Windows</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          Not yet available. A Windows build is in development. Nobody has yet watched it land a
          commit on a Windows machine, because there is no Windows machine here; the macOS and
          Linux builds each got exactly that on the MacBook and on the Debian box before release,
          and Knot does not ship what it has not verified. When it passes, it lands here and on
          the <a href="/knot/changelog/" class="text-accent hover:underline">changelog</a> first.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 04 Verify a download ─────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section id="verify" class="relative mb-16 scroll-mt-24">
        <SectionEyebrow num="04" label="VERIFY A DOWNLOAD" align="left" />
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-primary mb-4">Verify your download</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed mb-3">
          Every release publishes its SHA-256 checksums. Comparing takes one command and proves
          the file you hold is byte-for-byte the file in the release.
        </p>
        <p class="mb-1 text-xs font-mono text-hint uppercase tracking-widest">macOS</p>
        <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface border border-border rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed text-xs mb-3"><code>shasum -a 256 ~/Downloads/Knot-macos.dmg</code></pre>
        <p class="mb-1 text-xs font-mono text-hint uppercase tracking-widest">Linux</p>
        <pre class="text-primary/80 bg-surface border border-border rounded-lg p-3 overflow-x-auto leading-relaxed text-xs mb-3"><code>sha256sum ~/Downloads/knot-vcs-amd64.deb</code></pre>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-surface border border-border font-mono text-[11px] space-y-1.5">
          <div class="text-hint uppercase tracking-widest text-[10px] mb-1">Current release checksums</div>
          <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-16 shrink-0">macOS</span><span class="text-primary/60 break-all">{DMG_SHA}</span></div>
          <div class="flex gap-3"><span class="text-hint w-16 shrink-0">Linux</span><span class="text-primary/60 break-all">{DEB_SHA}</span></div>
          <div class="text-hint pt-1">Build-stamped filenames ({DMG_FILE} · {DEB_FILE}) are attached to the release too, the same bytes under names that record which build they came from.</div>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed mt-3">
          No CI runner sits in the chain: these builds are compiled on one laptop and
          uploaded to GitHub Releases by hand.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- ── 05 First save ────────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
      <section id="first-save" class="relative mb-8 scroll-mt-24">
        <SectionEyebrow num="05" label="YOUR FIRST SYNCED SAVE" align="left" />
        <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-primary mb-4">Check that it worked</h2>
        <ol class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed space-y-2 list-decimal pl-5 mb-4">
          <li>Protect a folder (the welcome's last step, or <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">knot solo</span> in a terminal).</li>
          <li>Open any file in that folder, in any editor. Change something. <span class="text-primary/80">Hit save.</span></li>
          <li>Watch the dashboard. A debounce runs first, so it is not instant. What lands is a real commit signed by your device identity, and plain <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">git log</span> can read it.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="text-sm text-secondary leading-relaxed">
          To sync with a second machine: install Knot there too, then pair the two with a
          one-time code from the Devices tab. After that the saves cross on their own: on a shared LAN
          the two machines talk straight to each other, while over Tor it is a lot slower.
          On both routes the bytes are Noise-encrypted and each side checks the other's Ed25519
          key before anything moves.
          When both sides edited and the histories diverged, Knot merges them itself. The merge commit
          is built deterministically, from sorted parents and a fixed identity, so both machines arrive
          at the same commit id rather than each inventing its own. What it will not do is guess at a
          real conflict: if the same lines changed on both sides, or the incoming side would overwrite
          edits you have not committed, it stops. The push says
          <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">push refused: not a fast-forward</span>, the other
          side waits under <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">refs/knot-incoming/</span>, and
          <span class="font-mono text-primary/80">knot incoming</span> shows you what is parked.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="mt-14 pt-8 border-t border-border text-center text-xs text-hint space-y-2">
        <p>
          Stuck? <a href="mailto:mail.roshankumargupta@gmail.com?subject=Knot%20install" class="text-accent hover:underline">Write in</a>
          with your OS version and what the terminal printed.
        </p>
        <p>
          <a href="/knot/" class="text-accent hover:underline">← Knot</a> ·
          <a href="/knot/#download" class="text-accent hover:underline">Downloads</a> ·
          <a href="/knot/changelog/" class="text-accent hover:underline">Changelog</a>
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </main>

  <Footer />
</Base>
